\par\par \uc2 TIMES INSIDER\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Look at Our Future Work Lives\b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 November 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the pandemic drags on, so does the profound reordering of work and office life. After a year without commutes, many white-collar workers have grown accustomed to the flexibility of working from home. Companies are reassessing whether they need to rent large office spaces with so few employees coming in. A record number of United States workers quit their jobs in September as the ''Great Resignation'' continues, while thousands more are protesting pay or working conditions. In a recent interview for the Coronavirus Briefing newsletter, Emma Goldberg, the new future of work reporter at The Times, shares insight into how the shifting tides of the pandemic will shape our work lives. Jonathan Wolfe\par \par \uc2 What does your role look like?\par \par \uc2 It's an exciting beat because it's very broad -- it encompasses everything from the social dynamics of the workplace and the design of the office to whether the office itself has a future. It covers everything from what productivity means and how it's assessed, to the public health implications of our return to offices.\par \par \uc2 Where do things stand with return to office?\par \par \uc2 A lot of companies were supposed to be back in their offices in July or September, but with the Delta variant's recent surge, there were a lot of postponements. Now, there are companies that are saying they're going to be back in offices in early 2022. There's a lot of uncertainty right now. That said, the Biden mandates should make a lot of larger employers and workers feel safer.\par \par \uc2 What trends will shape our work lives in the coming years?\par \par \uc2 A big one is the question of flexibility. During the pandemic, a lot of companies had to face the fact that some of the norms of the office that have been so fundamental for years aren't necessary to produce great work output. In fact, they were making things really challenging for some people. That forced a reckoning among employers about what kind of flexibility could be afforded to workers. Another trend is the war for talent. Now, there is a big labor shortage, with record high rates of people leaving their jobs, which makes talent a scarce commodity. That's pushing companies to rethink what perks they're offering, from raises to other kinds of benefits.\par \par \uc2 There's also continued conversations about what companies can do to support the movement for racial justice. Last summer, there was a wave of commitments from companies promising to rethink their diversity, equity and inclusion strategies. Now, a little over a year later, workers are following up and asking how much of it really panned out and what companies are going to do to make good on the initial promises they made.\par \par \uc2 How has the pandemic changed work culture?\par \par \uc2 Companies that went remote or hybrid over the last year had to rethink what it means to build culture when workers aren't sitting next to each other. For some companies, it has meant organizing virtual holiday parties. Others are having their employees do virtual hikes together, meaning they each separately go on walks but call each other up while doing so. And still others are finding that their workers miss being together in person, and they're coming back to their desks to hang out even though it's not required.\par \par \uc2 What about long-term changes to our work lives?\par \par \uc2 Something I heard from a lot of people over the last year is that the pandemic has forced a reckoning with the premium people place on their careers and on the role of work in their lives. It's forced them to question what really makes them happy and how to build a balance between work and life that feels more sustainable. This mentality could prompt a huge shift in how much time and energy we devote to our jobs, or how we draw boundaries between work and life.\par \par \uc2 Follow the @ReaderCenter on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for more coverage highlighting your perspectives and experiences and for insight into how we work.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has forced many companies to reconsider office norms of the past. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEENAH MOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211124ehbo0004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
