\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Here to Help; Answers to Questions About the Biden Vaccine Mandate\b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has released details of its plan to prevent the spread of the coronavirus in the workplace by requiring private-sector workers to get vaccinated by Jan. 4 or do weekly testing. Here are answers to some of the questions readers submitted. Emma Goldberg\par \par \uc2 WHAT IS IN THE \uc2 OCCUPATIONAL SAFETY AND HEALTH ADMINISTRATION\uc2 'S NEW RULE? Private companies of 100 or more employees must require their workers to be fully vaccinated by Jan. 4 or submit to weekly coronavirus testing and wear masks in the workplace. The deadline for employers to enforce the mask mandate is Dec. 5. All employees, including those working part-time, count toward the 100-worker threshold. Independent contractors do not.\par \par \uc2 DOES THE NEW RULE APPLY TO REMOTE WORKERS? Remote workers, people who work exclusively outdoors and those who report to workplaces without other people don't have to get vaccinated or submit to weekly testing. OSHA is focusing specifically on ensuring people's protection against the coronavirus in the workplace. Employees who work part of the time in the office and the rest of the time remotely are required to follow the rule.\par \par \uc2 CAN EMPLOYEES OPT FOR TESTING INSTEAD OF GETTING VACCINATED? It will be up to companies to determine whether their workers can opt out of getting vaccinated by submitting to testing. If workers choose to be tested instead of being vaccinated, they must also wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 WILL EMPLOYEES BE PAID FOR THE TIME IT TAKES TO GET VACCINATED? WHAT ABOUT FOR GETTING BOOSTERS? Employers have to provide up to four hours of paid time off for their workers to get vaccinated, as well as sick leave for them to recover from side effects. They are required to provide this leave starting Dec. 5. Booster shots aren't currently required under OSHA's rule. Employers are not required to provide leave for testing, given that the vaccine is safe and highly effective.\par \par \uc2 CAN EMPLOYERS FIRE WORKERS WHO DON'T COMPLY? When it comes to people who do not have a medical or religious exemption, employers are generally free to discipline those who don't follow their rules.\par \par \uc2 For more information on the vaccine mandate for private employers, go to nytimes.com/business.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY JIM WILSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211106ehb60004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
