\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Researching Real-Life Situations to Test Big Theories\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeanna Smialek \par \uc2 980 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 David Card, Joshua Angrist and Guido Imbens were honored for delving into cause and effect in ways that shed light on labor markets.\par \par \uc2 David Card has made a career of studying unintended experiments to examine economic questions -- like whether raising the minimum wage causes people to lose jobs.\par \par \uc2 Joshua D. Angrist and Guido W. Imbens have developed research tools that help economists use real-life situations to test big theories, like how additional education affects earnings.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, their work earned them the 2021 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences.\par \par \uc2 All three winners are based in the United States. Mr. Card, who was born in Canada, works at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 . Mr. Angrist, born in the United States, is at the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 , and Mr. Imbens, born in the Netherlands, is at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Sometimes, nature, or policy changes, provide situations that resemble randomized experiments,'' said Peter Fredriksson, chairman of the prize committee. ''This year's laureates have shown that such natural experiments help answer important questions for society.''\par \par \uc2 The recognition was bittersweet, many economists noted, because much of the research featured in the prize announcement was co-written by \uc2 Alan B. Krueger\uc2 , a Princeton University economist and former White House adviser who died in 2019. The Nobels are not typically awarded posthumously. Despite that note of sadness, the economics profession celebrated the news, crediting the winners for their work in changing the way that labor markets in particular are studied.\par \par \uc2 ''They ushered in a new phase in labor economics that has now reached all fields of the profession,'' Trevon D. Logan, an economics professor at Ohio State, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  shortly after the prize was announced.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Card's work has challenged conventional wisdom in labor economics -- including the idea that higher minimum wages led to lower employment. He was a co-author of influential studies on that topic with Mr. Krueger, including one that used the border between New Jersey and Pennsylvania to test the effect of a minimum wage change. Comparing outcomes between the states, the research found that employment at fast food restaurants was not negatively affected by an increase in New Jersey's minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Card has also researched the effect of an influx of immigrants on employment levels among local workers with low education levels -- again finding the impact to be minimal -- and the effect of school resource levels on student education, which was larger than expected.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm sure that if Alan were still with us, that he would be sharing this prize with me,'' Mr. Card said in a news conference, after recognizing Mr. Krueger's contributions. He also noted that initially, when it came to the minimum wage study, ''quite a few economists were quite skeptical of our results.''\par \par \uc2 David Neumark\uc2 , an economist at the University of California, Irvine, who co-wrote a paper contesting Mr. Card and Mr. Krueger's findings in the minimum wage study, said he still thought the work had data issues -- but added that there was no doubt that the methodology was important.\par \par \uc2 ''They've all done great work -- they've changed the way that labor economists do research,'' Mr. Neumark said of the three winners.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Angrist and Mr. Krueger tried in the early 1990s to gauge how much benefit people derive from extra years of education. To figure it out, they took advantage of the fact that students born earlier in the year can legally leave school earlier than those born later in the year. Those born earlier tended to get less education and also earned less later on. The effect of an additional year of education, they estimated, was a 9 percent increase in income.\par \par \uc2 That study helped spur the additional work on research methods that Mr. Angrist and Mr. Imbens later carried out. That contribution has reshaped the way researchers think about and analyze natural experiments, according to the Nobel committee.\par \par \uc2 The pair showed that it was possible to identify a clear effect from an intervention in people's behavior -- like a subsidy that might encourage people to ride bicycles to work -- even if a researcher could not control who took part in the experiment, and even if the impact varied across individuals. They also came up with a transparent framework for such research that has increased trust in it.\par \par \uc2 ''The challenge, for me, has always been trying to understand, when people do empirical work, what exactly the methodological challenges are,'' Mr. Imbens said via telephone in a news conference for the announcement.\par \par \uc2 Two American economists affiliated with Stanford, \uc2 Paul R. Milgrom\uc2  and \uc2 Robert B. Wilson\uc2 , won the 2020 Nobel in economics for improvements to auction theory. \uc2 Abhijit Banerjee\uc2  and \uc2 Esther Duflo\uc2  of M.I.T. and \uc2 Michael Kremer\uc2  of \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  won in 2019 for their experiment-based research in development economics.\par \par \uc2 The award, formally called the Bank of Sweden Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel, has been given out since 1969.\par \par \uc2 Because the award is announced in the middle of the night on the United States' West Coast, two of this year's recipients were woken up by phone calls from Sweden informing them of their prize.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Imbens said he was asleep when he received the call from the prize committee -- around 2 a.m. -- and was ''absolutely stunned'' to hear the news. He said he was pleased to win it alongside friends, noting that Mr. Angrist was the best man at his wedding.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Card thought that a friend of his -- whom he identified only as Tim -- was pulling a prank on him, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''But then the phone number actually was a Swedish phone number,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The Nobel in economics was announced in Stockholm on Monday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CLAUDIO BRESCIANI/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211012ehac0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
