\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Here to Help; How I Hold It Together\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jaspal Riyait \par \uc2 389 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the art director for Well, working from home has encouraged me to redefine how I spend my days. Here are six things I've been doing to practice ''self care.'' Jaspal Riyait\par \par \uc2 I'M REALISTIC ABOUT WHAT I CAN ACHIEVE IN A DAY. I used to make lengthy to-do lists for daily errands, work and more. But lately, those tasks feel overwhelming. Now, I knock out just one task per day.\par \par \uc2 I MOVE MY BODY. Pandemic life has wreaked havoc on my body, with lots of tension in my lower back and hips. To counteract it, I find at least 15 minutes per day to roll out a yoga mat.\par \par \uc2 I GO ON EVENING WALKS. Just before the sun dips below the trees, I grab my dog's leash and head out for a walk. A late sunset means I can still get some vitamin D, and the fresh air feels therapeutic.\par \par \uc2 I CALL A FRIEND. My best friend, who lives in Canada, has been my closest confidant for more than a decade. Even though we lead busy lives, we make the effort to connect once a week with a good, old-fashioned phone call. During our hour together, we catch up, give each other advice and laugh. We've been each other's emotional rock through these challenging times, and I'm so grateful for our friendship.\par \par \uc2 I MAKE FANTASY TRAVEL PLANS. I may not be trekking across the world anytime soon, but that doesn't mean I can't fantasize about it. I like to imagine traveling to Laos and Cambodia. The picturesque landscapes give me hope.\par \par \uc2 I STICK TO A NIGHTLY ROUTINE. Like many others, I have been troubled by a touch of pandemic insomnia. Sticking to a nightly routine has been key to helping me get through it. I aim to go to bed at roughly the same time each night, and mindfully stretch my body with light, restorative yoga before slipping under the sheets. Once I'm tucked in, I take a series of long, slow breaths to help quiet my anxious mind. This combination, plus a CBD sleep aid, seems to do the trick.\par \par \uc2 For more wellness advice, go to nytimes.com/well.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY YOGEE CHANDRASEKARAN) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210912eh9c0006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
